WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.880
A practical SOC 2 readiness routine begins with commitments and scope.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.860
Define the service organization, the service or system being reported on,

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.680
customer commitments, relevant environments, dependencies,

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.580
and selected Trust Services Categories. Security is common.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.480
Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity,

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.340
and Privacy depend on service needs and promises.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:26.720
Good scope connects what customers rely on to the controls the organization operates.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.700
Next, map controls to criteria, risks, and commitments.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:34.840
A control should not exist only because it sounds audit friendly.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:39.940
It should support a defined objective. Access reviews support authorized access.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.880
Change management supports controlled system change.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.420
Monitoring supports detection and response.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.680
Vendor reviews support third-party risk management. Backup tests support recoverability.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:54.660
Policies support consistent expectations. Assign owners.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.520
Every control needs someone who understands what must happen, how often it must happen,

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.460
what evidence is required, where that evidence lives, and how exceptions are handled.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.520
Control ownership should be cross-functional where the work is cross-functional.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:13.300
Engineering, security, IT, HR, operations,

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.360
and vendor management may all contribute to the same SOC 2 program.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.340
Choose the report path honestly.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.400
Type I focuses on whether controls are suitably designed at a point in time.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:27.700
It can be useful for early readiness and customer assurance when the program is new.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.880
Type II focuses on design and operating effectiveness over a defined period.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:37.740
It usually requires stronger recurring evidence because the organization must show that

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.120
controls operated consistently over time. Prepare evidence workflows.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:47.460
Use ticketing, access review systems, monitoring tools, incident response records,

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.480
vendor review processes, backup tests,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.620
and change management workflows to create evidence as work happens.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.760
Do not wait until the review period ends to discover missing records.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:02.580
Evidence should be accurate, timely, traceable, and honest.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.360
Track exceptions and remediate gaps.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:09.340
A sustainable program does not pretend everything is perfect.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:16.120
It identifies issues, evaluates impact, assigns remediation, follows up, and improves.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:20.340
Communicate honestly with auditors and stakeholders at the appropriate level.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.080
Do not fabricate evidence or misrepresent control operation.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.620
The core message is simple: Type I proves design at a point in time;

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.560
Type II demonstrates operation over time.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:35.460
Both depend on scope, controls, owners, and evidence.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.600
The strongest SOC 2 programs treat readiness as normal operations,

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:41.740
not a last-minute audit event.
